<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Job hunting has two failure modes and most people alternate between
    them: forty applications in one desperate weekend, then nothing for a
    month. Neither works, and the second one is usually caused by the first.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's needed is a paced search — the right thing in the right order,
    each step small enough to actually do on a weeknight after work.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">In order</h2>
  <ol class="mt-6 space-y-4">
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">1 · What you've got</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Your real history, including the parts that don't fit a tidy
        narrative — the gap, the unrelated job, the thing you did for four
        years without a title for it.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">2 · The materials</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Written once properly rather than rewritten badly forty times. Then
        tailored per application — your actual history in this listing's
        language, with the genuine gaps named rather than papered over.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">3 · A cadence</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        A steady number of applications a week, on your list beside
        everything else in your life. Sustainable beats heroic, because the
        search that works is the one still running in week six.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Tailoring, without lying</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The rewriting is translation, not invention: what you did, in the words
    this employer uses for it. It won't add experience you don't have, and
    when the listing asks for something you can't offer it says so to you
    plainly rather than quietly fabricating a bullet point you'd have to
    defend in an interview.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Whatever kind of work</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    No assumed degree, no assumed profile on a networking site, no output
    written in the dialect of people who already have both. This has to work
    for a warehouse shift lead as well as it works for an office job, and
    that constraint shaped everything on this page.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">If it ended badly</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Losing a job is not primarily a job-search problem for the first
    fortnight — it's insurance, income, bills and being able to think. The
    app holds all of those, so that becomes one plan rather than four
    crises.
  </p>
</section>
